    "name": "Environmental Health",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/health/public-health/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Environmental health in Iran connects air quality, water and sanitation, climate hazards and the safety of health-care facilities. WHO’s evidence profiles show why exposure measurements, service indicators and announced adaptation plans must be interpreted separately. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Environmental health", "Air pollution", "Water and sanitation", "Climate adaptation", "Health facilities"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:32:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:32:44Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Environmental Health in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s environmental health risks, including air pollution, water and sanitation, climate adaptation and health-care facility resilience."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "environment-scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Health beyond the clinic",
        "content": "Environmental health examines how surroundings influence exposure to hazards and the conditions needed for health. WHO’s Iran overview identifies air pollution, drought, heat stress, water pollution and sand and dust storms among the country’s concerns. These risks cross administrative boundaries: protecting health can require action in energy, water management and infrastructure as well as medical services. [s1]\n\nThe same overview describes fossil-fuel combustion and dust storms as contributors to particulate pollution. These are distinct sources, so an effective response requires understanding the local mixture rather than assuming that every polluted location has the same problem. WHO describes work on health-impact assessment and planning for sand and dust storms; the existence of such activities does not establish that exposures have already declined. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-profile",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What an environmental profile measures",
        "content": "WHO’s 2025 country scorecard combines exposure indicators, estimated health impacts and policy information. Its publication year is not the observation year for every entry. The references identify different underlying years for particulate pollution, household energy and water services. Reading the whole document as a single measurement of conditions in 2025 would therefore be incorrect. [s2]\n\nA national annual mean for fine particulate matter describes broad exposure, rather than the air at a particular street or the severity of a short dust episode. Likewise, an estimated share of deaths attributable to pollution is different from a count of diagnoses directly recorded as pollution-related. The scorecard’s unavailable entries also matter: an absent value should remain unknown rather than becoming zero. These distinctions explain why no mixed-indicator trend chart is presented here. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "water-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Water, sanitation and service quality",
        "content": "WASH means water, sanitation and hygiene. WHO’s Iran overview describes work on household monitoring, health-care facilities and assessments of implementation. It also discusses urban and rural services separately, an important reminder that national coverage does not establish identical access everywhere. [s1]\n\nThe 2025 scorecard distinguishes safely managed drinking water from other service categories and identifies gaps in sanitation information. For readers comparing reports, a basic service indicator should not be substituted for a safely managed indicator. The scorecard also includes health-care facility standards and service gaps: environmental health concerns the places where care is delivered as well as conditions in households. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "adaptation-commitments",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Climate resilience: commitments and implementation",
        "content": "In its statement to the Seventy-seventh World Health Assembly, Iran described a strategic framework for 2023–2027 addressing environmental and occupational health in relation to climate change. The statement identified heatwaves, drought, floods, water scarcity and vector-borne disease among relevant hazards. Vector-borne diseases are infections transmitted by organisms such as mosquitoes. [s3]\n\nThe statement also announced ambitions for a climate-resilient health system by 2035 and net-zero health-sector emissions by 2060. These are government commitments, not independently verified achievements or forecasts. The same document requested technical and financial support and emphasized vulnerability assessment and monitoring. Its practical significance lies in the distinction between setting a destination and demonstrating that facilities, services and funding can deliver it. [s3]\n\nEnvironmental health progress consequently needs several kinds of evidence: exposure trends, reliable services and documented implementation. A policy announcement alone cannot substitute for those measurements. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {"id": "s1", "title": "Environmental and occupational health", "publisher": "WHO Regional Office for the Eastern Mediterranean, Iran country office", "url": "https://www.emro.who.int/iran/priority-areas/environmental-and-occupational-health.html", "language": "English", "publication_date": null, "access_date": "2026-10-28", "source_type": "country_programme_overview", "locator": "Air quality; Water, sanitation, and hygiene and waste management"},
      {"id": "s2", "title": "Health and environment scorecard: Iran (Islamic Republic of), update 2025", "publisher": "World Health Organization", "url": "https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/country-profiles/environmental-health/environmental-health-irn-2025.pdf?download=true&sfvrsn=407a527e_6", "language": "English", "publication_date": "2025-06-04", "access_date": "2026-10-28", "source_type": "environmental_health_country_profile", "locator": "Pages 1–3, indicators and definitions; page 4, references and underlying data years"},
      {"id": "s3", "title": "Statement of I.R. Iran on Climate Change & Health, agenda item 15.4 B", "publisher": "Delegation of Iran to the Seventy-seventh World Health Assembly; hosted by WHO", "url": "https://apps.who.int/gb/statements/WHA77/PDF/Iran-15.4.pdf", "language": "English", "publication_date": null, "access_date": "2026-10-28", "source_type": "government_statement", "locator": "Pages 1–2, strategic framework, commitments and requested support"}
